### 10. Item-wise expense splitting — CRITICAL REQUIREMENT

This is the main real-world problem-solving demonstration.

Scenario A:

Five friends visit a restaurant.

- Four people drink tea.
- One person orders expensive coffee instead.
- Everyone shares the samosas.
- Only four people should pay for tea.
- Only the coffee consumer should pay for coffee.
- All five should contribute to the samosas.

The application must calculate each person's contribution accurately.

Scenario B:

Five friends buy food.

- Four people consume tea.
- One person does not drink tea.
- All five consume samosas.
- Each item has its own price.
- The application must calculate the exact amount each person owes.

Implement an itemised bill interface where users can:

- Add multiple items.
- Enter item name and price.
- Set quantity.
- Select which members consumed each item.
- Split an item equally among selected members.
- Assign exact quantities or custom shares.
- Add applicable tax, discount and tip.
- Allocate shared charges transparently.
- Preview each member's contribution.
- Verify that the total allocated amount equals the bill total.

Example:

Tea: ₹100 / 4 consumers = ₹25 each.

Coffee: ₹120 / 1 consumer = ₹120.

Samosas: ₹150 / 5 consumers = ₹30 each.

Final:
- Four tea consumers pay ₹55 each.
- The coffee consumer pays ₹150.

The calculation engine must be accurate and tested.

Use integer paise internally rather than floating-point money calculations. Handle rounding differences explicitly and transparently.

